{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531","title":"Facilitator Notes for DTS","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting facilitator notes for dts to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"01","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/08"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_calibration","cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_ai_boundary"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Facilitator Notes for DTS\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Facilitator Notes For Dts, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-01-facilitator-notes-for-dts.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Facilitator Notes for DTS: Facilitator Notes For Dts, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA facilitator note is the written memory of a live decision. When a teacher runs a Design Tension Studio (DTS) session, the room produces opinions, energy, and a tentative plan; the facilitator note is what survives after the room empties. If that note only records that the group \"liked the AI tool,\" it preserves a mood, not a judgment. The professional skill in this section is writing notes that capture the value conflict the group actually wrestled with, the decision they negotiated, and the conditions attached to that decision, so that a colleague who was not in the room can audit and continue the work.\n\nThis matters because technology decisions in schools are rarely made once. They are revisited when a new family objects, when a device fails, or when an administrator asks why a tool was chosen. A facilitator note written to be defensible becomes the document that answers those later questions. A note written only to feel finished becomes a liability.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA 7th-grade humanities team meets to decide whether to adopt an AI essay-feedback tool. The conversation is lively and the team leaves saying yes. The facilitator writes: \"Team is enthusiastic; adopting the AI feedback tool next unit.\" Two weeks later a parent asks why their child's essay was read by an AI vendor, and the principal asks the facilitator what safeguards the team agreed to. The note answers neither question, because it recorded enthusiasm instead of the negotiated decision and its conditions.\n\nThe contrast worth studying is between a note that records what people felt and a note that records what the group committed to and on what terms. Only the second kind protects students and the teacher when the decision is later questioned.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA Design Tension Studio treats a technology decision as a conflict between two goods that cannot both be maximized at once. The facilitator's job is to keep both goods visible while the group negotiates a position between them. A facilitator note is strong when it captures four things that a tension map should contain:\n\n- **The two axes of the tension.** Name both goods in competition, for example \"faster, more frequent feedback for every student\" against \"students keep authorship and teachers keep authority over judgment.\" A note that names only one axis hides the conflict.\n- **Where the risk lands.** Identify which students bear the cost of the decision. English-language learners and students with IEPs are more likely to be misread by a generic essay model, so an equity cost that is invisible at the whole-class level becomes visible when you ask who is most affected.\n- **The negotiated position, not the preference.** Record the specific compromise the group reached, including any bounds placed on the tool, rather than the mood of the room.\n- **The evidence to collect next.** A negotiated decision is a hypothesis about practice. The note should name what would confirm or disconfirm it.\n\n| Note element | Weak version | Defensible version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tension | \"We talked about pros and cons.\" | Axis A (feedback speed) vs Axis B (authorship and teacher judgment). |\n| Risk location | \"Good for the class.\" | ELL and IEP students most likely to be misread; 6 flagged-for-support students named. |\n| Decision | \"Adopting the tool.\" | AI drafts comments; teacher reviews and signs each before release. |\n| Next evidence | (absent) | One-week sample comparing AI drafts to teacher-final comments. |\n\nThe course anchors sharpen these moves. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) keeps you asking whether the tool actually serves the content and pedagogy, not just the workflow. Critical edtech studies presses you to ask whose burden and whose data the tool quietly assumes. Together they turn a note from a record of consensus into a record of reasoning.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nConsider the difference between a facilitator note that polishes the meeting and one that preserves the decision. A first-pass note often reads:\n\n> Facilitator note: The AI essay tool is engaging and saves grading time. Recommend adopting it for the unit.\n\nThis is polish. It names a benefit and a recommendation but no tension, no risk, and no bound on the tool. A reader cannot tell what the team agreed to constrain.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: a facilitator note that names the tension\n\nScenario: a CAT 531 facilitator is running the Design Tension Studio with the 7th-grade humanities team that wants the AI feedback tool. The note must surface the value conflict, not hide it.\n\nBefore (polish without a named tension):\n\n> Facilitator note: The AI essay tool is engaging and saves grading time. Recommend adopting it for the unit.\n\nWalk the note against the studio map, one axis at a time:\n\n1. Name the value conflict. Axis A is \"faster, more frequent feedback for every student.\" Axis B is \"students keep authorship and teachers keep authority over judgment.\" The before note only names Axis A, so the tension is invisible.\n2. Locate who bears the risk. ELL students and students with IEPs are most likely to be misread by a generic essay model, so the equity cost lands unevenly. The before note treats the class as one undifferentiated group.\n3. Record the negotiated decision, not the preference. The team agrees the tool may generate draft comments, but the teacher reads and signs every comment before it reaches a student, and no comment is sent on flagged-for-support students without a human first pass.\n\nAfter (annotated facilitator note with the tension named and a negotiated decision):\n\n> Facilitator note (08.01): Value conflict on the studio map is feedback speed (Axis A) versus student authorship and teacher judgment authority (Axis B). Negotiated decision: AI drafts comments; teacher reviews and signs each one before release; comments on the 6 flagged-for-support students get a mandatory human first pass. Evidence to collect next: a 1-week sample of AI draft comments versus teacher-final comments to measure how often the teacher overrode the model.\n\nThe after note is stronger because a mentor teacher can audit it: the tension is on the map, the equity risk is located on named students, and the negotiated decision is something the team can be held to and can later test with evidence.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"What goes in the facilitator note?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The humanities team just voted yes on the AI essay tool. The room was warm and quick. As facilitator, what do you write first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Record the enthusiasm: the team loved it and is adopting it.\",\"next\":\"warm\",\"feedback\":\"Warmth is a mood, not a commitment. A later reader cannot audit what the team actually agreed to bound.\"},{\"label\":\"Name the two goods in conflict: feedback speed versus authorship and teacher judgment.\",\"next\":\"axes\",\"feedback\":\"Good. Naming both axes makes the negotiation visible instead of hiding it under agreement.\"}]},\"warm\":{\"prompt\":\"Two weeks later a parent objects and the principal asks what safeguards were agreed. Your note records only that the team was enthusiastic. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reconstruct the decision from memory.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Memory is exactly what the note was supposed to replace. The decision is now unverifiable.\"},{\"label\":\"Reopen the studio map and name the tension and bound retroactively.\",\"next\":\"axes\",\"feedback\":\"Better late than never, but the note failed its job: it should have captured this when the room was full.\"}]},\"axes\":{\"prompt\":\"Tension is on the map. Who in this class bears the cost if a generic essay model misreads writing?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The class as a whole benefits, so cost is shared.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"This hides where the risk lands. ELL and IEP students are more likely to be misread; the cost is uneven.\"},{\"label\":\"ELL and IEP students; name the flagged-for-support students and require a human first pass.\",\"next\":\"bound\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Locating the risk on named students is what turns an equity concern into a checkable safeguard.\"}]},\"bound\":{\"prompt\":\"You have the tension and the risk. What makes the decision defensible rather than a vague endorsement?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Write that the team approved using AI for feedback.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Too broad. The team approved a bounded use, not unconditional adoption.\"},{\"label\":\"Write the bound: AI drafts, teacher signs each comment, mandatory human first pass for flagged students, plus the evidence to collect next.\",\"next\":\"end_strong\",\"feedback\":\"This is a note a colleague who missed the meeting can hold the team to.\"}]},\"end_weak\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the note feels finished but cannot answer a challenge. It records a mood, not a negotiated decision with conditions.\",\"feedback\":\"A facilitator note that cannot survive a parent or principal question is a liability.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_strong\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the note records the value conflict, the located risk, the bounded decision, and the next evidence. It survives a challenge.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the defensible note: tension, bound, risk, and evidence plan all inspectable.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why recording the negotiated decision and its bound protects students and the teacher better than recording that the team was enthusiastic.\" answer=\"The negotiated decision and its bound state exactly what the tool is and is not allowed to do, on which students, and with what human checkpoint, so a later reader (parent, principal, colleague) can audit and enforce the agreement. Recorded enthusiasm preserves only a mood; it cannot tell anyone what safeguards were promised, so it leaves both students and the teacher unprotected when the decision is questioned.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen writing a facilitator note, put the named value conflict first, the tool's affordance second, and the policy boundary third. A decision is only as defensible as the conditions attached to it. Before you close the note, confirm it answers three questions a later reader will ask:\n\n1. **Tension check:** What two goods were in conflict, and where did the group land between them?\n2. **Risk check:** Which students bear the cost of this decision, and what protects them?\n3. **Bound check:** What is the tool explicitly not allowed to do, and what evidence will test whether the decision held?\n\nA note that cannot answer all three may feel complete but will not survive a challenge.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A facilitator writes: 'Team agreed to use AI feedback; everyone was on board.' Which question does this note fail to answer?\" explanation=\"The note records agreement but states no bound: it does not say what the tool is explicitly not allowed to do, so it fails the bound check and cannot be enforced later.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The tension check, because it names no two goods in conflict.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The bound check, because it states no condition on what the tool may not do.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Nothing; agreement plus enthusiasm is a complete note.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The risk check only, since it names the team but not the tool.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most frequent error is mistaking enthusiasm for agreement. A room can feel unanimous about adopting a tool while disagreeing entirely about how it should be bounded, and a note that records the warmth erases that unfinished negotiation. A second error is recording the decision without its conditions, which lets the tool drift toward uses no one actually approved. The note should make the boundary as visible as the choice.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake a concrete situation: your team has agreed to pilot an AI feedback tool, and you are the one writing the note that goes into the shared unit folder. Begin by stating the tension in the team's own terms, not in abstract language, so a colleague recognizes the real argument. Then write the negotiated decision as a rule someone could follow without you in the room: who does what, in what order, and with what human checkpoint.\n\nNext, attach the bound. The team did not approve \"using AI for feedback\"; it approved \"AI drafts, teacher signs, mandatory human first pass for six named students.\" That precision is what separates a defensible note from a vague endorsement. Finally, name the evidence that will tell you whether the decision was sound, so the note points forward to verification rather than closing the question prematurely.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a facilitator note the way an auditor would. Ask whether you could reconstruct the meeting's real decision from the note alone, and whether you could tell a skeptical parent exactly what the tool is and is not permitted to do. As you read your own draft, keep a two-column check: on the left, the claim the note makes; on the right, the condition or evidence that makes the claim inspectable. A strong note lets a reader who missed the meeting hold the team to its commitments without needing anyone to explain them again.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA facilitator note reaches the standard when a mentor teacher can see the value conflict, the bounded decision, and the named risk without asking a follow-up question. Visual neatness is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are the tension, the bound, and the evidence plan. If any of those is missing, mark the gap with an annotation and ask BigAL for a targeted comparison between your note and a defensible exemplar rather than a general explanation of facilitation. A useful closing line reads: \"I recorded the negotiated decision and its bound because the team's agreement was conditional, and I left out the suggestion to adopt the tool unconditionally because no one in the room actually approved that.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same note to a high-stakes setting: a self-contained special-education classroom where the AI essay model is more likely to misread student writing. The tension axes stay the same, but the risk axis dominates, and the negotiated decision should shift toward a heavier human first pass or no AI feedback on certain students at all. Rewriting the note for this setting, rather than copying it, is the test of whether you understand facilitator notes as judgment rather than as a template.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-01-facilitator-notes-for-dts-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 08.01. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your note records the value conflict, the bounded decision, the named risk, and the evidence to collect next. That is what lets a facilitator note answer a challenge instead of merely closing a meeting.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","stem":"A facilitator note reads: \"The AI essay tool is engaging and saves grading time, so adopt it.\" On a Design Tension Studio map, what is the strongest objection?","options":["The note should also mention how much money the tool costs.","The note names only the benefit axis (speed) and leaves the competing value, student authorship and teacher judgment, off the map.","The note is too short to be a real facilitator note.","The note should have been written by the AI tool itself."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A Design Tension Studio map has to show both axes of the conflict. Naming only the benefit hides the value being traded away.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_one_sided_tension","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A team decides an AI tool may draft essay comments. Which line turns that preference into a negotiated decision a mentor teacher could later audit?","options":["The team agreed the tool was a good idea.","Everyone liked the tool, so we will use it this unit.","AI drafts comments, but the teacher reads and signs each one before release, with a mandatory human first pass on flagged-for-support students.","We will see how it goes and decide later."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A negotiated decision states a commitment the team can be held to and later test, not just that they liked the tool.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_preference_not_decision","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"When mapping the equity risk of the AI essay tool, what does a defensible facilitator note do?","options":["States the tool carries the same risk for every student equally.","Names the specific students most likely to be misread (ELL students, students with IEPs) so the equity cost is located, not averaged.","Leaves equity out because the tool is only a draft assistant.","Assumes the vendor already handled fairness."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Equity risk lands unevenly. A defensible note locates who bears it rather than treating the class as one undifferentiated group.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_uniform_risk","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_facilitator_notes_for_dts_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A facilitator justifies adopting the tool entirely on the grounds that \"students find it fun.\" Why is this not yet a defensible decision on the tension map?","options":["Fun is the only thing that matters in a classroom decision.","Engagement alone is not a justification; the note must tie the choice to a protected learning value and a named risk.","The decision is fine as long as test scores are not mentioned.","The note simply needs to be longer to be defensible."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Engagement matters but does not replace evidence about learning, access, or risk. The map must connect the choice to a protected value and a named cost.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_engagement_as_justification","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_facilitator_notes_for_dts","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-02","cat531-supplement-08-01-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_one_sided_tension","pattern":"naming_only_one_axis_of_the_tension","description":"Writing a Design Tension Studio map that names only the benefit axis (for example feedback speed) and leaves the competing value (authorship, teacher judgment) off the map","trigger":"facilitator note recommends a tool without naming the value it trades against","feedback":"A tension map needs both axes. Name the competing value the tool puts at risk, not only the benefit it offers.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_uniform_risk","pattern":"treating_the_class_as_one_undifferentiated_group","description":"Assuming a technology choice carries the same risk for every student instead of locating who bears the equity cost (ELL students, students with IEPs)","trigger":"note describes the class as a single group with no subgroup risk","feedback":"Locate who bears the risk. Name the specific students for whom the tool is most likely to misfire.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_preference_not_decision","pattern":"recording_a_preference_instead_of_a_negotiated_decision","description":"Logging that the team liked or chose a tool rather than recording the negotiated decision the team can be held to and later test","trigger":"map ends with a preference and no commitment or evidence plan","feedback":"Record the negotiated decision and what evidence would later confirm it, not just which tool the team preferred.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_1_dts_engagement_as_justification","pattern":"engagement_as_justification","description":"Defending a classroom tool because it is engaging rather than because it protects students and improves a specific learning activity","trigger":"the only stated reason for the tool is that students find it fun","feedback":"Engagement is not a justification on its own. Tie the choice to a protected learning value and a named risk.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"84f7ccb4260090f194a55fe6f79f40ffeee54da8950f4597fa8695a6080015fb","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}